  "query_preview": "..After today the upper air pattern becomes \nprogressive as the ridge moves east and a series of Pacific \nlows move through the region. Today will be similar to yesterday\nwith highs 2-4 degrees above normal and southeasterly winds. \n\nFriday into the weekend a longwave trough will move over the\nwestern CONUS with two embedded vort maxes. One will be on the\nsouthern end of the main trough and travel through Baja and\nSonora Friday into Saturday. The second will be further north\ntraveling through the Great Basin and the Four Corners region.\nAt the same time moisture increases with PWAT values 150-200\npercent of normal. Scattered showers begin Friday afternoon \neast of Tucson spreading over most of southeast Arizona through \nthe late afternoon and evening. Friday models also show some \ninstability (100-400 J/kg), so some isolated thunderstorms will \nbe possible. Saturday will see rain chances mainly from Tucson \nnorth and eastward. Thunder looks less likely Saturday than \nFriday. Neither trough is positioned the best for heavy rainfall\nwith mean QPF 0.01\"-0.3\" and localized areas up to 0.60\" in \nthunderstorms through Saturday night. Highest rainfall totals \nwill be along the international border, mountains, and northern \nGraham/Greenlee Counties. Most of this rainfall is expected to \nfall on Friday. This weather system will also bring breezy winds\nFriday and Saturday and usher in cooler temperatures with highs\n6-10 degrees below normal this weekend into next week.\n\nSunday will be a transition day as shortwave ridging briefly \nforms before the next Pacific low moves through the region.\nModels have slowed down this next trough compared to 24 hours\nago. Ensembles bring it through southern California and northern\nBaja Tuesday then through Arizona Wednesday. There is some \nspread in the models mainly with how fast it moves through, so\nexpect more timing changes. Because the low has slowed down,\nMonday now looks to be dry with rain chances beginning early\nTuesday morning and continuing into Wednesday morning. This low\nlooks to be on a better trajectory to deliver more rainfall \nthan the first two, since the core of the low moves through \nArizona rather than the to the north or south. Rain totals are \ncurrently 0.1\"-0.6\" Tuesday into Wednesday with the lowest rain \ntotals in Cochise County and highest north of Tucson as well as \nover central/western Pima County. Also, snow levels may come \ndown low enough for some flurries at the top of Mt Graham and \nWhite Mountains, but no accumulation is expected.",
